WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.824
In the Yongle era of the Ming dynasty, Beijing became an important political center, and the Forbidden City was also built in the early fifteenth century.

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:14.558
The Yongle Emperor mobilized more than a million craftsmen, and it took fourteen years to complete this imperial palace.

00:00:14.908 --> 00:00:22.732
The Forbidden City has 980 buildings and more than 8,000 rooms; it is the largest palace complex in the world.

00:00:23.082 --> 00:00:29.466
Across the Ming and Qing dynasties, a total of twenty-four emperors handled state affairs here.

00:00:29.816 --> 00:00:38.480
The palaces are arranged neatly along the central axis, with red walls and yellow tiles, symbolizing the dignity of imperial power and political order.

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:46.486
The golden glazed tiles shine brightly in the sunlight, making the palace even more magnificent and grand.

00:00:46.836 --> 00:00:55.620
In 1925, this place became the Palace Museum; in 1987, it was inscribed as a World Cultural Heritage site.

00:00:55.970 --> 00:01:00.650
Today, the Forbidden City is a cultural heritage site that everyone can visit.
